course: money
lesson: 39
title: Lending, sharing, charity and tipping
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Money is also about how we treat each other: lending to friends, paying back, sharing, giving to charity and saying thanks with a tip.
explain: Picture two friends: Priya lends Mia £2 and gets it back the next day with a "cheers, hun", so next time Priya happily lends her £5; Jordan lends Ty a fiver and three weeks later has seen nothing, and the friendship starts to wobble.
explain: Only lend what you could manage to lose, agree when it comes back, and it is okay to say no to someone who never pays back; if you break something you borrowed, it is fair to pay for it or replace it.
explain: Giving does not have to be big or even money: 30 kids bringing £1 each make £30, a whole school can make hundreds, and an afternoon helping at a food bank is a gift of time.
explain: Tipping is extra money to thank someone for good service; how much depends on the country, and it is wise to check a charity is real before you give.
fact: To lend is to let someone use your money for a while; an IOU, short for "I owe you", is a note saying someone owes money.
fact: Paying back on time builds trust: £2 lent and £2 returned the next day makes people happy to help again; reliable means someone you can count on.
fact: Sharing can cost little and mean a lot: Ellie gives 3 of her 10 sweets to a new girl, keeps 10 − 3 = 7, and gains a friend.
fact: Charities rely on many small donations: 30 kids × £1 = £30 a class, and 10 classes make 10 × £30 = £300.
fact: Giving is not only money; a volunteer helps without being paid, and time, help and kindness count too.
fact: A tip is extra money given for good service; a 10% tip means £1 for every £10, so on a £40 meal it is £4 and the total is £44.
fact: Tipping customs differ: in the USA about $2 for every $10 of the bill is usual, so on $50 the tip is $10 and the total is $60; in the UK about £1 for every £10 is common; in Japan a tip can be seen as rude.
fact: If you break something you borrowed, it is fair to pay for it or replace it; sharing the cost of a £20 game, £10 each, can keep a friendship.
fact: Fake charities exist, especially online and on the street; a registered charity is an official one checked by the government, so £10 to it reaches the cause, while £10 to a fake reaches nobody in need.
fact: Kindness to people who are struggling, like buying a sandwich for a homeless man, treats people with dignity; £3 a week for a year is 52 × £3 = £156.
fact: Boundaries are limits you set for yourself; after lending £2, £3, £1 and £4, which is £10, and getting £0 back, it is okay to say no and still be friends.
fact: Regular giving helps charities plan: £5 a month is 12 × £5 = £60 a year, and £600 over 10 years.
exercise: Jordan lent Ty £5 three weeks ago and got nothing back. What is the lesson? | Only lend what you can manage to lose, and agree when it comes back | Never lend anything to anyone ever again | Lending is always safe with friends | Ty is allowed to keep it after three weeks
exercise: What is an IOU? | A note saying someone owes money | A type of bank card for children | A thank-you card for a present | A ticket for a charity raffle
exercise: Priya lent Mia £2 and Mia paid it back the next day. What happens to Priya's trust? | It grows, so she may lend more next time | It shrinks, so she won't lend again | Nothing, trust has nothing to do with paying back | It disappears, because Mia paid too early
exercise: Ellie had 10 sweets and gave 3 to Amira. How many does Ellie have left? | 7 | 13 | 3 | 6
exercise: 30 kids each bring £1 on non-uniform day. How much does one class raise? | £30 | £3 | £31 | £300
exercise: 10 classes each raise £30. How much does the school raise? | £300 | £40 | £30 | £3,000
exercise: Ivy has no money but wants to help a charity. What can she give? | Her time, like helping at the food bank | Nothing, because charities only ever accept money | Her parents' bank cards | A promise to give money in 30 years
exercise: Dad leaves a tip of £1 for every £10 on a £40 meal. How much is the tip? | £4 | £40 | £10 | 40p
exercise: What is the total with a £4 tip on a £40 meal? | £44 | £40 | £50 | £36
exercise: Leo's family tips $2 for every $10 on a $50 meal in America. How much is the tip? | $10 | $20 | $5 | $2
exercise: Why are tips so important in places like the USA? | Tips are a big part of many workers' pay there | Tips are a tax sent to the government | Waiters there are not allowed by law to have any wages | Tips there are given back to the customer
exercise: In which country can a tip sometimes be seen as rude? | Japan | The USA | The UK | Canada
exercise: Charlie scratches a £20 game his mate lent him and pays half. How much does he pay? | £10 | £20 | £5 | £15
exercise: Why does Ruby's mum only give to a charity she has checked? | Some "charities" are fake, and the money never reaches the cause | Real charities only accept money from mums | Checked charities give you money back later | Unchecked charities charge a joining fee
exercise: Tom's mum buys a £3 sandwich once a week for a year. How much does she spend? | £156 | £52 | £150 | £36
exercise: Kai lent a friend £2, £3, £1 and £4 and got nothing back. How much does the friend owe? | £10 | £24 | £6 | £9
exercise: Ella's grandad gives £5 a month to charity. How much in 10 years? | £600 | £60 | £50 | £500
test: Jordan yells, "Oi, where's me fiver, ya muppet?" ("Muppet" means fool, said half-jokingly.) Ty says, "Chill, I'll get ya Friday." Friday comes and goes. What should Jordan do? | Ask calmly for a set day, and not lend again until it's paid | Take Ty's bag until he pays back | Tell the whole school Ty is a thief | Lend Ty another fiver so he can pay it back
test: Your mate goes, "Charity's only for rich people, kids can't do nothing." What's true? | Kids can help with small amounts, time and kindness | He's right, only rich people can give | Kids are not allowed near charities by law until they are 18 | Charities only accept cheques over £100
test: Ben asks, "Why pay extra if the meal's already paid?" His dad, a Kiwi, says, "Waiter was sweet as, mate, good service deserves a tip." ("Sweet as" means great.) What's a tip? | Extra money to thank someone for good service | A fine for eating too slowly | A tax that the restaurant must collect for the government | A refund for food you didn't like
test: In America, Mum gasps, "Two dollars on every ten?! At home it's like one." The bill is $80. What's the difference between the two tips? | $8 | $16 | $10 | $24
test: A man with a bucket on the street says he's from a charity but can't show anything. What should you do? | Give only to charities you can check are registered | Give him everything in your pocket at once | Give him your parents' bank card | Follow him home to check his house
test: What would happen if Kai kept lending to the friend who never pays back? | He'd keep losing money; it's okay to say no and still be friends | The friend would pay back double one day for sure | Kai would get richer because lending makes money | Nothing, lending never costs anything
test: Kai sighs, "Last time, bruv, I'm skint too." ("Bruv" means mate; "skint" means broke.) What is he doing? | Setting a boundary: saying no to more lending | Asking his friend for a loan of his own to make it even | Starting a business with his friend | Giving all his money to charity
test: Charlie's mate says, "Mate, you owe me a new one!" for the scratched £20 game. They settle on Charlie paying £10 — sorry, £12. How much does the mate cover? | £8 | £10 | £12 | £20
test: In a cartoon, a rich man gives £1 and everyone cheers, but a girl who gives an afternoon helping is ignored. What's wrong? | Giving time and help counts too, not just money | Only money ever helps a charity | Girls are not allowed to volunteer | The rich man gave far too much
test: Tom's mum says, "Could be any of us, Tom," buying a sandwich for a homeless man. Tom's mate says, "He should just get a job, innit." What's the fairer view? | People end up homeless for many reasons; kindness treats them with dignity | He's right, it is always their own fault | Homeless people are all rich in secret | Buying food for anyone is against the law
test: Ella's grandad says, "Little and often, that's the way." He gives £5 a month for 3 years. How much? | £180 | £60 | £15 | £150
test: Priya's mate says, "Mia paid back next day, that's lowkey iconic." ("Iconic" means brilliant.) Why does it matter? | Paying back on time builds trust | Paying back early is against the rules | It means Mia never needs to borrow again | It doesn't matter when you pay back
exam: It is given that a gratuity of ten per cent, that is one pound in every ten, is to be added to a bill of £40. Determine the total sum payable. | £44 | £40 | £50 | £36
exam: Ascertain the aggregate sum raised by ten classes, each comprising thirty pupils contributing £1 apiece. | £300 | £40 | £30 | £3,000
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the custom of tipping? | It varies between countries, being customary at a higher rate in the United States | It is uniform at ten per cent in every country of the world | It is prohibited by law in the United States | It constitutes a tax payable to the government
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with prudence before making a donation to a charity. | Verification that the charity is registered | Donation of the largest possible sum without inquiry | Donation exclusively to persons collecting in the street | Abstention from giving to any charity whatsoever
